# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, which rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout. The committed blobs here are already pure LF, so
# nothing in history is wrong -- but Prettier checks the *working tree* and
# defaults to endOfLine:lf, so every file Git converted on the way out failed
# `format:check` while being perfectly fine in the repo. That made the check
# useless as a signal: real formatting errors sat inside a wall of false ones.
#
# eol=lf pins the working tree to match the repo, for every clone, without
# anyone having to set core.autocrlf on their machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Detection is good but not perfect, and a single misdetected binary is a
# corrupted file rather than a noisy diff. These are declared outright.
*.webp binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
